AB  - Team-based Mobile Crowd Sensing (MCS) is a prevalent data collection paradigm for large-scale sensing tasks, where workers communicate and cooperate in teams, effectively mobilizing an adequate workforce for task execution. In this context, recruiting trustworthy teams while safeguarding each team’s data privacy is one of the most critical issues. However, the contradiction between data evaluation and privacy preservation presents a significant challenge, as data evaluation seeks to utilize as much information as possible, while privacy preservation aims to conceal it. Moreover, online recruitment of unknown teams faces the exploration-exploitation dilemma, where exploration identifies uncertain teams, and exploitation recruits already identified trustworthy teams. Additionally, team trustworthiness frequently exhibits non-stationarity, changing erratically due to diverse factors, rendering its evaluation substantially more complex. To address these challenges, we propose a Trusted and Privacy-Preserving Truth Discovery scheme, named TPTD, for quality-enhanced reliable data collection in team-based MCS. Firstly, we introduce a team-based secure data integration mechanism to acquire reliable data while preserving privacy. Meanwhile, the team recruitment is formulated as a Combinatorial Multi-Armed Bandit problem, applying trustworthiness-based Thompson Sampling to balance exploration-exploitation. Lastly, we propose a consistency-reliability-based team identification mechanism to mitigate the impact of trustworthiness instability. Experiments show that our TPTD scheme outperforms existing methods by 17.3% in data collection and by 25% in team identification.
