AB  - In the field of machine learning, many problems can be formulated as the minimax problem, including reinforcement learning, and generative adversarial networks, to name just a few. As a result, the minimax problem has garnered significant attention from researchers in recent decades. However, there has been relatively little work on theoretically studying the privacy implications of the general minimax paradigm. This paper focuses on the privacy aspects of the general minimax setting by integrating differential privacy with the minimax problem. Additionally, leveraging algorithmic stability theory, we theoretically analyze the high-probability generalization performance of the differentially private minimax algorithm under the strongly-convex-strongly-concave condition. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first time that the high-probability generalization performance of the general minimax paradigm has been analyzed, rather than specific tasks such as adversarial learning or AUC maximization, while taking into account the impact of differential privacy.
ER  - 

