AB  - Integrating encryption into Machine Learning (ML) pipelines allows organizations to leverage data-driven intelligence without compromising user privacy, ensuring confidentiality across data storage, transmission, and computation. However, encrypting data during both training and testing phases introduces significant computational overhead. To mitigate this cost, this paper analyses a hybrid paradigm: training models on plaintext data and performing inference on encrypted data. While Homomorphic Encryption (HE) enables secure computation, it often requires modifying ML algorithms to be compatible. This study evaluates ML performance metrics, including accuracy, inference time, latency, and throughput, by benchmarking multiple HE schemes across diverse datasets. Our dual-advantage approach reduces training overhead while maintaining flexibility in selecting encryption-ML pairs. The results show that Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) offers better privacy protections than Partial Homomorphic Encryption (PHE). However, it comes with higher computational costs, including longer delays and lower processing speed. These trade-offs should be considered according to the performance needs of the specific machine learning application. This research, in the broader development of artificial intelligence, not only provides a systematic guide based on evidence for the matching between different machine learning algorithms and cryptographic schemes, but also pushes forward the transformation of trustworthy AI from theory to engineering practice.
