AB  - Graph-based and flat statistical feature spaces are both used for operational anomaly detection, yet how complementary they are—and how well detectors built on them transfer across deployments—remains largely untested. We study both questions for Internet inter-domain routing (BGP). We combine a graph-topological view and a statistical view in a multi-view detector and evaluate cross-domain transfer with a factorial design (two autonomous systems, two route collectors) and CORAL domain adaptation over 12 directed transfer pairs. A complementarity analysis confirms that the two views carry predominantly orthogonal signals, motivating their fusion, whose benefit is then verified by a single-view-versus-fusion ablation. Our central finding is that transfer difficulty is driven by topology asymmetry, not observation-point distance: detection holds within a single AS (macro-F1 up to 0.878) but collapses across structurally different ASes (0.40–0.70). This yields a topology-driven model-selection hypothesis—flat classifiers (MLP) for sparse, hierarchical ASes and message-passing graph networks (TopoGPS) for dense peering meshes—paired with per-detection feature attribution for interpretability. On the April 2025 Iberian power outage, it reaches 75% recall at a 1.3% false-alarm rate. The analysis methodology—not a single trained model—targets other domains that pair relational graphs with event streams.
