AB  - Integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in agriculture has drastically changed the way of decision-making. It also enabled precision farming and sustainable agricultural practices. Though the traditional AI techniques depend on centralized data aggregation, the modern scenario has raised concerns over data privacy, security, and computational efficiency. Federated Learning (FL) can be defined as a decentralized AI paradigm. It offers a privacy-preserving alternative by enabling collaborative model training across distributed data sources without transferring raw data. In this survey, we comprehensively analyze FL applications in agriculture. We categorize its use in crop monitoring, yield prediction, disease detection, and precision farming. We explore key challenges, including data heterogeneity, communication overhead, security vulnerabilities, model convergence issues, and emerging solutions such as blockchain integration, differential privacy, and edge computing. Moreover, we critically compare existing survey studies and identify gaps in the literature. Our findings highlight the potential of FL in transforming smart agriculture while addressing its limitations through interdisciplinary advancements. This survey could be a strong foundational reference for the study of researcher-practitioners in the field of technology-driven agriculture. Based on this foundation, the future work can aim to develop scalable, secure, and efficient decentralized AI solutions for the agricultural sector.
