AB  - Zero-shot anomaly detection (ZSAD) based on vision-language models has shown strong cross-category transferability, making it attractive for open-world inspection scenarios. In this work, zero-shot refers to zero target-domain supervision: DynaCLIP is trained on labeled source-domain anomaly data and evaluated on unseen target categories and datasets without target images, labels, or masks during training. However, when CLIP is directly applied to this setting, it often aligns global object semantics more reliably than it aligns subtle local defects, leading to insufficient pixel-level localization accuracy. To address this issue, we propose DynaCLIP, a dynamic CLIP-based framework that performs sample-adaptive semantic alignment at inference time. DynaCLIP contains three tightly coupled components: (i) Localized Attention Enhancement (LAE), which re-focuses visual attention on defect-relevant regions; (ii) Adaptive Prompt Alignment (APA), which generates dynamic text–visual prompts conditioned on each input image; and (iii) Hierarchical Semantic Aggregation (HSA), which consolidates multi-scale anomaly cues by selecting the most anomalous patches and semantically grouping them with K-Means. On MVTec AD, DynaCLIP obtains 91.7%/96.4% image-level AUROC/AP and 93.3%/85.6% pixel-level AUROC/PRO; on VisA, it obtains 86.1%/89.9% image-level AUROC/AP and 96.0%/87.2% pixel-level AUROC/PRO. It also achieves 98.40% AUROC and 99.65% AP on a real-world high-speed railway defect dataset, showing practical potential for railway inspection. The code is available at https://github.com/cloudesean/DynaCLIP_.
