T1  - Knowledge-guided federated intelligence for explainable and privacy-aware multimodal decision systems
AU  - Dwivedi, Ashish Kumar
JO  - Knowledge-Based Systems
VL  - 352
SP  - 117034
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/10/25/
SN  - 0950-7051
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.knosys.2026.117034
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950705126017600
KW  - Knowledge-based systems
KW  - Federated learning
KW  - Multimodal decision systems
KW  - Explainable AI
KW  - Privacy-aware intelligence
AB  - The deployment of intelligent decision systems in distributed, privacy-sensitive environments demands learning paradigms that combine robustness, transparency, and semantic grounding. While federated learning enables collaborative training without sharing raw data, this study proposes a knowledge-guided federated intelligence (KG-Fed) framework for explainable, privacy-aware multimodal decision systems. The approach integrates structured domain knowledge, including symbolic rules, relational knowledge graphs, and semantic constraints, directly into the federated learning process, guiding local optimization, regulating global aggregation, and enabling semantically grounded explanations. By operating at the knowledge level rather than the raw data level, the framework reduces direct information exposure while enabling consistent reasoning across heterogeneous clients. A formal convergence analysis shows that KG-Fed converges to a stationary point at the same asymptotic rate as standard federated averaging, with an explicit, bounded additive term arising from cross-client knowledge divergence. Evaluation on three multimodal decision benchmarks shows that the benefit of knowledge-guided regularization is dataset-dependent: it yields a statistically significant performance improvement over data-driven federated baselines on a chemical sensor drift task, a positive but non-significant trend on human activity recognition, and no measurable gain on a tabular census-income task. Across all three datasets, however, knowledge integrated during training produces substantially higher constraint-satisfaction rates than the same knowledge applied post hoc, indicating more faithful, intrinsic explanations. These results position knowledge-centric design as offering consistent explainability benefits alongside performance gains that vary with data modality, informing when the added computational cost is worthwhile.
