AB  - Conventional approaches to chemical discovery rely heavily on human expertise and experimental trial and error. This makes the process slow and resource-intensive when exploring large chemical spaces and designing novel compounds. Artificial intelligence (AI) has transformed this field by enabling predictive, generative, and increasingly autonomous approaches. In particular, machine learning, deep learning, generative AI, and autonomous laboratories allow systems to predict chemical outcomes, design molecules and synthetic routes, and perform experiments in closed loops. Agentic AI extends these capabilities by allowing AI systems to plan multistep tasks, use external tools, interpret experimental results, and adapt subsequent actions to pursue scientific objectives. These capabilities are being integrated into autonomous AI systems across multiple fields, including organic synthesis, nanochemistry, computational chemistry, robotics, and autonomous experimentation. Together, these developments support a comprehensive review of agentic AI and its potential to enable autonomous chemical discovery. However, existing reviews have primarily examined organic synthesis and nanochemistry separately, without a unified framework for how agentic AI can integrate these domains toward autonomous discovery. In this review, we integrate advances in predictive and generative AI, agentic systems, autonomous laboratories, and related computational and robotic technologies, while distinguishing capabilities demonstrated experimentally from those proposed for future applications. We also examine the major challenges that must be addressed to achieve reliable autonomous discovery, including model interpretability, data quality, reproducibility, experimental validation, scalability, safety, and governance.
