UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2542660526002520
KW  - Federated learning
KW  - Continual learning
KW  - Prompt learning
KW  - Zero-day intrusion detection
KW  - Network security
KW  - Internet of things
AB  - Zero-day intrusion detection in federated learning remains challenging because models must detect previously unseen attacks while coping with non-IID client data, privacy constraints, and catastrophic forgetting during continual adaptation. Existing federated intrusion detection systems only focus on closed-set classification, communication efficiency, or robust aggregation, but they provide limited support for continual adaptation to emerging attacks without sharing raw traffic data. This paper proposes FedPromptZero, a continual federated prompt-tuning framework for detecting and adapting to previously unseen network attacks. Built on a compact transformer encoder that is frozen after an initial warm-up round, so that subsequent updates are confined to a lightweight prompt bank and classifier head, FedPromptZero combines dynamic prompt expansion for representing emerging attack patterns, differentially private prototype rehearsal for retaining class-level knowledge, and novelty-aware aggregation for prioritising informative and reliable client updates. The framework is evaluated on the UNSW-NB15, N-BaIoT, and CICIoV2024 datasets under non-IID federated settings, where selected attack classes are excluded from initial training and subsequently introduced as previously unseen attacks, with results confirmed across nine alternative held-out-class configurations and three federated learning seeds. Experimental results show that FedPromptZero outperforms four generic federated optimization baselines (FedAvg, FedProx, SCAFFOLD, FedNova) and Zero-X, a specialized zero-day federated baseline reproduced under an identical protocol, on both closed-set classification and open-set detection across all three datasets. Relative to the generic baselines, FedPromptZero improves Accuracy by up to 3.36 percentage points and Macro-F1 by up to 3.13 percentage points, and improves zero-day detection AUROC by up to 10.09 percentage points; relative to Zero-X, the strongest baseline evaluated, it still improves Accuracy by up to 1.11, Macro-F1 by up to 0.81, and AUROC by up to 1.92 percentage points. These gains are obtained while nearly halving per-round communication cost and keeping the forgetting rate at most 0.31%, and maintaining a formally characterised privacy–utility trade-off, with a composed ε ≈ 10.2 guarantee achievable at a moderate noise level for under 1.1 percentage points of AUROC cost. Overall, FedPromptZero provides an accurate, adaptive, communication-efficient, and privacy-aware solution for continual federated zero-day intrusion detection.
