AB  - This study investigates the forensic relevance of the Modularer Infotainment Baukasten version 2 (MIB2) infotainment system in a 2016 Volkswagen Golf Mk7, presenting a non-invasive approach for data acquisition and analysis that does not require hardware disassembly. The objective is to locate and document key forensic artifacts, such as navigation history, stored routes, contact-related metadata, Bluetooth pairing logs, and granular trip data. In addition, the research introduces three modules for the open-source Vehicle Logs Events And Properties Parser (VLEAPP) forensic toolkit, designed to partially automate tasks typically carried out by forensic practitioners. In contrast to prior work that has emphasized physical extraction techniques or proprietary solutions, the methodology outlined here is low-cost, and accessible to practitioners without specialized hardware, is shown to recover navigation history, contact and Bluetooth metadata, granular trip data, and volatile call records from crash dumps. The paper also describes a controlled crash procedure in the infotainment operating system, triggered by running an application incompatible with the infotainment hardware. This intentional incompatibility causes a crash and produces a crash dump that may expose runtime memory artifacts, namely from a paired smartphone.
