AB  - Generative artificial intelligence (Gen AI) has considerable potential to enhance supply chain resilience (SCR), but its effective adoption remains subject to technological uncertainty. This study integrates BERTopic, the Supply Chain Operations Reference (SCOR) model, intuitionistic fuzzy sets (IFS), and clamped intuitionistic fuzzy cognitive map (IFCM) simulation to develop a patent-driven framework for screening and prioritizing potential Gen AI applications. Based on 139,661 Gen AI patent abstracts associated with Chinese listed firms, the framework identifies 870 fine-grained technology topics, maps them onto 29 SCOR-oriented nodes, and uses patent-text evidence to construct a technology propagation structure and a 29×5 technology-performance allocation matrix. Four main findings emerge. First, patent portfolio analysis focuses on enabling, data-processing, and infrastructure capabilities, with semantic evidence linking these capabilities to SCR dimensions such as reliability, responsiveness, agility, cost, and asset management. Second, counter- and unresolved evidence reveal constraining pathways, with negative propagation concentrated in limited cross-node relationships. Third, sustained support for selected driver nodes creates selective spillovers rather than uniform gains; under the soft-clamped scenario, engineering quality and progress management, order lifecycle management, and intelligent logistics path planning achieve the highest non-driver activation. Fourth, SCOR contributions depend jointly on steady-state activation and patent-semantic associations, so strong semantic support alone does not guarantee high modeled contribution. Overall, the framework provides patent-evidence-based decision support for screening candidate technologies, prioritizing staged evaluation, and comparing conditional SCOR performance profiles.
