KW  - Functional manifestation
KW  - Double pincer movement
KW  - Human-AI symbiosis
KW  - Threshold governance
AB  - Friedrich Hayek’s premise regarding the incomputability of tacit knowledge faces fundamental challenges in the digital intelligence age. While the literature focuses on artificial intelligence (AI) efficiency or human-machine collaboration, it overlooks the underlying power dimension, failing to systematically extend the analysis to the macro-social order. Addressing this gap, this study proposes the human-AI epistemic topological model to reveal the relativity of knowledge, characterizing knowledge structure through two orthogonal dimensions: algorithmic computability (dead labor) and human epistemic agency (living labor). This study argues that AI does not understand tacit knowledge but bypasses understanding through a mechanism of functional manifestation. It identifies a double pincer movement: algorithms force the computability threshold downward via probabilistic fitting, while human subjects outsource the epistemic agency threshold rightward due to convenience incentives. This displacement precipitates a topological reconstruction: the hollowing of explicit reason, deprivation of embodied experience, and preoccupation of the ontological void by algorithmic prediction. Consequently, this micro-epistemic shift macroscopically manifests as planned spontaneous order, a governance form that pre-prunes possibility spaces using predictive technologies. Finally, the study traces civilizational trajectories from primitive chaos to the digital intelligence resurgence and proposes Centaur Civilization as a normative reconstruction. Threshold governance is advocated to constitutionalize the knowledge order, preserving the institutional space for incomputable generative knowledge against the hegemony of algorithmic schemata.
